1
00:00:01,540 --> 00:00:05,040
NASA's Space Launch System rocket and Orion spacecraft form

2
00:00:05,040 --> 00:00:08,910
the backbone for the agency's Artemis missions to the Moon.

3
00:00:09,320 --> 00:00:09,960
Over development,

4
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:13,720
the rocket and spacecraft moved from design and manufacturing

5
00:00:14,400 --> 00:00:15,060
to testing,

6
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:16,200
assembly,

7
00:00:16,300 --> 00:00:17,480
and integration,

8
00:00:17,620 --> 00:00:20,480
with some hardware delivered to the launch pad at Kennedy.

9
00:00:21,120 --> 00:00:24,380
The SLS core stage was built at Michoud Assembly Facility

10
00:00:24,980 --> 00:00:28,640
— the largest rocket stage NASA has built since the Saturn V,

11
00:00:29,300 --> 00:00:30,520
212 feet tall,

12
00:00:31,080 --> 00:00:33,900
with four RS-25 engines.

13
00:00:34,020 --> 00:00:37,030
SLS will be the most powerful rocket ever built,

14
00:00:37,180 --> 00:00:41,160
powered by two solid rocket boosters and four RS-25

15
00:00:41,160 --> 00:00:44,220
engines providing over 8 million pounds of thrust.

16
00:00:45,540 --> 00:00:49,900
SLS rolled out to Launch Complex 39B at Kennedy and was stacked

17
00:00:49,900 --> 00:00:53,580
inside the Vehicle Assembly Building ahead of its first flight.

18
00:00:54,120 --> 00:00:55,800
At 1:47 a.m.

19
00:00:56,880 --> 00:01:02,100
EST (6:47 UTC) on November 16,

20
00:01:02,320 --> 00:01:02,660
2022,

21
00:01:02,760 --> 00:01:05,920
NASA's Orion spacecraft launched atop the SLS rocket

22
00:01:05,920 --> 00:01:09,620
from historic Launch Pad 39B at Kennedy Space Center.

23
00:01:10,620 --> 00:01:12,600
The liftoff officially began the Artemis

24
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:15,320
I mission — an uncrewed flight test.

25
00:01:16,020 --> 00:01:19,140
Artemis I was the first integrated test of the agency's

26
00:01:19,220 --> 00:01:23,220
deep space exploration systems: the SLS rocket,

27
00:01:23,220 --> 00:01:24,620
the Orion spacecraft,

28
00:01:24,820 --> 00:01:25,840
and supporting ground systems.

29
00:01:27,140 --> 00:01:28,840
It was the first in a series of

30
00:01:28,840 --> 00:01:31,760
increasingly complex missions to the Moon.

31
00:01:32,300 --> 00:01:33,990
Orion was sent on a path to the Moon,

32
00:01:34,140 --> 00:01:37,620
beginning a 25.5-day mission around the Moon.

33
00:01:37,780 --> 00:01:41,800
This was an uncrewed flight test demonstrating the SLS rocket's

34
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:44,980
ability to carry Orion around the Moon and back to Earth.

35
00:01:46,100 --> 00:01:47,170
Over the course of the mission,

36
00:01:47,700 --> 00:01:49,950
Orion performed two lunar flybys,

37
00:01:50,080 --> 00:01:54,340
coming within 80 miles (129 kilometers) of the lunar surface.

38
00:01:55,500 --> 00:01:57,550
At its farthest distance during the mission,

39
00:01:58,120 --> 00:02:03,660
Orion traveled nearly 270,000 miles (435,000 kilometers)

40
00:02:04,300 --> 00:02:05,620
from Earth.

41
00:02:05,840 --> 00:02:08,480
NASA's Orion spacecraft completed a parachute-assisted

42
00:02:08,560 --> 00:02:11,980
splashdown in the Pacific Ocean at 9:40 a.m.

43
00:02:12,780 --> 00:02:15,420
PST (12:40 p.m.

44
00:02:15,560 --> 00:02:18,640
EST). Splashdown marked the final major

45
00:02:18,640 --> 00:02:21,640
milestone of the Artemis I mission.

46
00:02:22,060 --> 00:02:24,360
Artemis I set new records of performance,

47
00:02:25,020 --> 00:02:26,620
exceeded efficiency expectations,

48
00:02:27,260 --> 00:02:30,880
and established new safety baselines for humans in deep space.

49
00:02:31,780 --> 00:02:33,240
Following Artemis I,

50
00:02:33,400 --> 00:02:38,040
human beings will fly around the Moon on Artemis II. Under Artemis,

51
00:02:38,700 --> 00:02:40,380
NASA will land the first woman and

52
00:02:40,380 --> 00:02:42,610
the first person of color on the Moon.

53
00:02:43,300 --> 00:02:44,000
SLS,

54
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:44,420
Orion,

55
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:47,130
and the Gateway in lunar orbit form NASA's

56
00:02:47,260 --> 00:02:49,150
backbone for deep space exploration.

57
00:02:50,400 --> 00:02:53,030
SLS is the only rocket that can send Orion,

58
00:02:53,940 --> 00:02:54,440
astronauts,

59
00:02:54,500 --> 00:02:56,480
and supplies to the Moon in a single mission.

